module CommentRunCrossesSeparator exposing (..)

-- Rule C1 -- one gap is one attachment, and the run moves together -- asked of
-- the unrecorded `,` / `|` that rule C2 sends a comment ACROSS.
--
-- A single-line `{- -}` written in a list's comma gap leads the item BELOW it
-- (C2); a `--` and a multi-line `{- .. -}` both stay with the item above (C2's
-- line-leading exception, and "a comment that brings its own rows stays put").
-- Each of those is right on its own, and `leadsAcrossItemSeparator` asked the
-- question per COMMENT -- so a gap holding one of each tore in half, the two
-- comments ending up on opposite sides of a separator the author wrote them on
-- one side of.
--
-- `[ 1 {- a -} -- b` came out as `[ 1 -- b` / `, {- a -} 2`: the run REVERSED.
-- The movability test is now asked of the whole run (`gapRunCrossesTogether`),
-- which is the same all-or-nothing C3 already applies to riding.
--
-- Found 2026-08-06 by `fuzz-idempotency.py --mix`, the run-COMPOSITION axis.
-- `--run N` splices N copies of one kind and can never build a mixed run, so it
-- swept clean at n=1, 2 and 3 over the same gaps.


crossedUnderLineComment =
    [ 1 {- a -} -- b
    , 2
    ]


crossedUnderMultiline =
    [ 1 {- a -} {- b
                   two -}
    , 2
    ]


-- The same gap in a record update, whose separator is the unrecorded `|` and
-- whose "previous item" is the base name.


crossedInUpdate =
    { rec {- a -} -- b
        | fld = 1
    }


-- THE BOUNDARY, and it is what keeps the fix from being "comments never move".
-- A run whose members ALL cross still crosses together, exactly as before -- the
-- rule is unanimity, not the presence of a second comment.


homogeneousRunStillCrosses =
    [ 1
    , {- a -} {- b -} 2
    ]


-- And one comment alone is untouched: plain C2, the rule this fixture exists to
-- leave standing.


singleCommentStillCrosses =
    [ 1
    , {- a -} 2
    ]


-- The mirror of the first case: the stayer written FIRST. The output is in
-- source order either way, so the reordering oracle is blind here -- this half
-- was found by reading the grid, not by a gate.


tornWithoutCrossing =
    [ 1 {- a
           two -} {- b -}
    , 2
    ]
